P("cardiac_cycle","Cardiac cycle",0.12,["cardiovascular_system"],"One heartbeat: relax and fill, then squeeze and eject.","Each heartbeat has two phases: the heart relaxes and fills with blood, then it squeezes to push blood out. This takes under a second.","The beat begins in the SA node. Pressure differences open and close the valves, creating the 'lub-dub' sounds.","Phases: atrial systole, isovolumetric contraction, ejection, isovolumetric relaxation, rapid and reduced filling. LV pressure peaks ≈ 120 mmHg; end-diastolic volume ≈ 120 mL, end-systolic ≈ 50 mL; Wiggers diagram links ECG, pressures and volumes.",[("Cycle length","≈ 0.8 s at 75 bpm"),("EDV / ESV","≈ 120 / 50 mL"),("Ejection fraction","≈ 55-70%")],"The heart's four phases occur in under one second, and its valves open and close about 100,000 times daily.",
[("Electrical start","The SA node in the right atrium fires, and the wave of excitation spreads across both atria.",["sa_node","right_atrium","left_atrium"]),
 ("Atria squeeze","The atria contract and top up the ventricles with the last 20-30% of their filling.",["right_atrium","left_atrium","tricuspid_valve","mitral_valve"]),
 ("Pause at the AV node","The impulse is delayed about 0.1 s at the AV node so the ventricles can fill, then races down the septum and Purkinje fibers.",["av_node","interventricular_septum"]),
 ("Ventricles tense","Ventricular contraction raises pressure; the AV valves slam shut ('lub') while all valves are briefly closed.",["tricuspid_valve","mitral_valve","left_ventricle","right_ventricle"]),
 ("Ejection","When ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, the aortic and pulmonary valves open and blood is ejected.",["aortic_valve","pulmonary_valve","aorta","left_ventricle"]),
 ("Relaxation","The ventricles relax, the semilunar valves close ('dub'), and pressure falls.",["aortic_valve","pulmonary_valve"]),
 ("Filling and coronary flow","The AV valves open and blood flows in; the coronary arteries fill mainly now.",["tricuspid_valve","mitral_valve","coronary_arteries"])],"19.3",kn=("blood_circulation",),mol=["atp"])
P("breathing","Breathing and gas exchange",0.25,["respiratory_system"],"Muscles move air in and out; oxygen and carbon dioxide swap with the blood in the alveoli.","To breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and the chest expands, drawing air into the lungs. Oxygen crosses into the blood and carbon dioxide leaves, then you breathe out.","Pressure inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure when the chest expands, so air flows in. Exhalation at rest is passive recoil.","Boyle's law drives ventilation. Alveolar PO2 ≈ 100 mmHg and PCO2 ≈ 40 mmHg; venous blood arrives at PO2 ≈ 40 and equilibrates in ≈ 0.25 s. Hemoglobin carries ≈ 98% of O2; CO2 travels mostly as bicarbonate. Brainstem chemoreceptors respond mainly to CO2/pH.",[("Tidal volume","≈ 0.5 L"),("Alveolar PO2","≈ 100 mmHg"),("Rate","≈ 12-16/min")],"Red blood cells spend less than a second in a pulmonary capillary, yet that's enough to fully load oxygen.",
[("Inspiration","The diaphragm contracts and flattens, and external intercostals lift the ribs; lung volume grows and pressure drops.",["diaphragm","ribcage","lung_r","lung_l"]),
 ("Air travels down","Air is warmed and filtered through the nose, then passes through larynx, trachea and bronchi.",["nose_airway","larynx","trachea","bronchi"]),
 ("Reaching the alveoli","Branching bronchioles deliver fresh air to millions of alveoli.",["bronchioles","alveoli"]),
 ("Diffusion across the barrier","O2 diffuses into capillary blood and CO2 diffuses out, across a barrier thinner than a micrometer.",["alveoli","capillary","red_blood_cell"]),
 ("Oxygen transport","Oxygen binds to hemoglobin in red cells and is carried to tissues, which release it where CO2 and acidity are higher.",["red_blood_cell","blood"]),
 ("Expiration","Muscles relax, elastic recoil of lungs pushes air out carrying CO2.",["diaphragm","lung_r","lung_l"]),
 ("Brain control","The brainstem measures blood CO2 and pH and adjusts breathing rate and depth.",["brainstem"])],"22.3",kn=("respiratory_physiology","heme_oxygen"),mol=["oxygen","carbon_dioxide","4hhb","heme_b","bicarbonate"])
P("action_potential","Action potential (nerve impulse)",1e-4,["nervous_system"],"A brief, self-propagating electrical pulse that carries signals along a neuron.","A neuron at rest is electrically charged. When stimulated enough, a spike of voltage races down its axon like a wave.","Sodium channels open, sodium rushes in and flips the voltage; then potassium channels open and restore it. Myelin makes the signal jump quickly.","Threshold ≈ -55 mV, peak ≈ +30 mV, resting ≈ -70 mV; Hodgkin-Huxley kinetics; Na+ channel inactivation gives refractory period; saltatory conduction at nodes of Ranvier reaches ≈ 100 m/s in large myelinated axons.",[("Resting","≈ -70 mV"),("Peak","≈ +30 mV"),("Duration","≈ 1-2 ms"),("Speed","≈ 0.5-120 m/s")],"Despite the electrical name, the 'wire' is salty water, and ions, not electrons, carry the signal.",
[("Resting state","Na+/K+ pumps and leak channels hold the inside about 70 mV negative.",["neuron","cell_membrane"]),
 ("Reaching threshold","Incoming inputs depolarize the axon hillock; at about -55 mV voltage-gated Na+ channels open.",["neuron"]),
 ("Rising phase","Na+ floods in, reversing the voltage to about +30 mV.",["neuron","cell_membrane"]),
 ("Falling phase","Na+ channels inactivate and K+ channels open; K+ leaves and the membrane repolarizes.",["neuron"]),
 ("Refractory period","Overshoot below rest briefly prevents another spike so the impulse travels one way.",["neuron"]),
 ("Propagation","Local currents depolarize the next patch; myelinated axons jump between nodes of Ranvier.",["neuron","peripheral_nerves"])],"12.4",kn=("action_potential",),mol=["acetylcholine"],cert="C1")
P("synaptic_transmission","Synaptic transmission",1e-6,["nervous_system"],"How one neuron passes a message to the next using chemicals.","An electrical pulse reaching a nerve ending triggers release of chemical messengers that cross a tiny gap and excite or inhibit the next cell.","Calcium entering the terminal makes vesicles fuse and release neurotransmitter, which binds receptors on the target cell. Chemical synapses can strengthen or weaken with use.","Vesicle fusion via SNARE complexes and synaptotagmin (Ca2+ sensor) takes ≈ 0.2-0.5 ms; ionotropic (AMPA, NMDA, GABA-A) versus metabotropic receptors; reuptake and enzymatic degradation terminate signals; LTP/LTD underlie plasticity.",[("Synaptic cleft","≈ 20 nm"),("Delay","≈ 0.5 ms"),("Vesicle","≈ 40 nm")],"A single cortical neuron can receive thousands of synaptic inputs.",
[("Arrival","An action potential reaches the axon terminal.",["neuron"]),
 ("Calcium entry","Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ rushes in.",["neuron","cell_membrane"]),
 ("Release","Vesicles fuse with the membrane and spill neurotransmitter into the cleft.",["neuron"]),
 ("Binding","Neurotransmitter molecules bind receptors on the next cell, opening ion channels.",["neuron","cell_membrane"]),
 ("Response","Excitatory inputs depolarize, inhibitory ones hyperpolarize the receiving cell.",["neuron"]),
 ("Cleanup","Neurotransmitter is taken back up or broken down; astrocytes help clear excess.",["astrocyte"])],"12.5",kn=("synapse_plasticity",),mol=["acetylcholine","dopamine","gaba","serotonin"],cert="C1")
